  "meta": {
    "title": "a*b*c* — three phases, joined by ε",
    "blurb": "Some a’s, then some b’s, then some c’s, any of them possibly none. Each phase is one looping state; the ε-moves let the machine move on without reading anything. Only the last state accepts, yet ε itself is accepted — through the ε-closure.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "abc",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aacc",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbbc",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "ba",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "cab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "abca",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "epsilon-closure",
        "concatenation",
        "textbook"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "The textbook example for **ε-closure**. The start state's closure is all three states, so the empty word is accepted even though the start state itself is not accepting.\n\nRemoving the ε-moves (Algorithms ▸ ε-NFA → NFA) makes every state accepting and adds shortcut edges — a→b, a→c and b→c on the letters that would have followed. The DFA adds a dead state for words like **ba** that break the order."
    }
  }
}
